          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1992,
          "date_decided": "1992-03-25",
          "holding": "The Court held that the Adoption Assistance and Child Welfare Act's requirement that States make 'reasonable efforts' to prevent removal and reunify families creates no privately enforceable right under § 1983 or directly under the Act.",
          "relevance": "It is the controlling authority that federal child welfare performance requirements are enforced administratively through funding conditions, not by private suit.",
          "quote": "We hold that the Act does not create an enforceable right on behalf of respondents.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112712/suter-v-artist-m/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112712
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re Interest of DeWayne G., Jr.",
          "citation": "263 Neb. 43, 638 N.W.2d 510 (2002)",
          "court": "Nebraska Supreme Court",
          "year": 2002,
          "date_decided": "2002-01-25",
          "holding": "The court held that Neb. Rev. Stat. § 43-283.01 gives a parent no right to demand a separate pre-termination hearing on whether reasonable efforts to preserve and reunify the family were made, and reinstated the termination of parental rights.",
          "relevance": "It is Nebraska's construction of the state-law codification of the federal Title IV-E reasonable-efforts condition on funding.",
          "quote": "the plain language of § 43-283.01 contains no such directive granting a parent or any other party the right to bring a motion requesting a \"separate hearing\"",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1810106/in-re-dewayne-g-jr/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1810106
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re Interest of Walter W.",
          "citation": "274 Neb. 859, 744 N.W.2d 55 (2008)",
          "court": "Nebraska Supreme Court",
          "year": 2008,
          "date_decided": "2008-01-18",
          "holding": "The court held that the ICWA and Nebraska ICWA 'active efforts' element must be proved by clear and convincing evidence rather than beyond a reasonable doubt, and that the Department met that burden, affirming the termination.",
          "relevance": "Failure to make and prove active efforts is the ICWA compliance failure most often raised on appeal in Nebraska.",
          "quote": "we decline to interpret § 1912(d) — and its Nebraska counterpart, § 43-1505(4) — as requiring the State to prove active efforts beyond a reasonable doubt",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2035819/in-re-interest-of-walter-w/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2035819
        }
      ],
